#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12591" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(1),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12592" :question/problem "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2,\\,2t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{(2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12608" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩=⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{8({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69a8244e-5abf-54e1-ba63-c2f8cd52909b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263908} {:answer/id #uuid "748ec60b-2955-5f11-81f0-9668c6f65596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264012} {:answer/id #uuid "bb21fe01-744f-557f-9ce1-a79d1b8712e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264627} {:answer/id #uuid "ea51054f-b1bb-5abc-b7fa-3fcacd6654a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265041} {:answer/id #uuid "f75aefb7-ea8f-5094-a4f8-d4beb0267428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea51054f-b1bb-5abc-b7fa-3fcacd6654a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265041} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efde9f4-a558-594d-aa01-2870e1f40df1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1522196c-85d3-5761-a4c8-6c5974238241" :question/math-academy-id "q-131959" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$ for $0\\le t<2\\pi ,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩ & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-3 \\\\ 4t=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-12i-9k \\\\  & =3(-4i-3k) \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =3\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =3(5) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6192c0d5-ad72-575b-8a2f-1a7e46fd57a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444290} {:answer/id #uuid "b474040b-15fe-5f81-9135-6af3cb2ab312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444302} {:answer/id #uuid "b8777a7b-55af-5c7c-909e-036d3340c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444303} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d75b37-7ea1-5e02-b969-0619510bbb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444304} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8f7d86-99fe-52e7-8a9e-f1de3df11de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6192c0d5-ad72-575b-8a2f-1a7e46fd57a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444290} :answer-field/id #uuid "af4cee66-027d-5ab7-8663-bfb226292ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "449eabe4-56aa-5632-8405-aab3edd3f407" :question/math-academy-id "q-131973" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\sqrt{3}t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨0,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,2t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,2(0),\\,3({0}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},0,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+0+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,6(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18914e3b-4c0c-57aa-b830-1660863a7e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263233} {:answer/id #uuid "5f58c6ad-9319-5d1b-9d99-ea47c8f0a8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263826} {:answer/id #uuid "80ef917a-5f07-5b72-a464-1b7b07712278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264108} {:answer/id #uuid "be495737-6290-535e-adb2-48549199f663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264653} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d65c24-d2d2-5f84-a217-8a8e9701cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d65c24-d2d2-5f84-a217-8a8e9701cc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265035} :answer-field/id #uuid "33500533-1ff2-52fe-b070-b355792f8c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7eb005e4-b7c8-57e9-a9a3-c7cb18e5c9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132067" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]k \\\\  & =[2{cos}^{2}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t]k \\\\  & =(2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t))k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2)k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={t}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0917bfa0-bfa9-5b16-886f-9cc52438c52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{4t+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444273} {:answer/id #uuid "499f4d91-d45c-54cb-8843-70596b66dfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444284} {:answer/id #uuid "602841f2-5541-552b-907f-100fdf2ade39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(4t+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444289} {:answer/id #uuid "ac65a6f7-9738-5d33-887a-9ad58c1f5490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444300} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e3162a-cf34-5775-9fc4-941bbc74028c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{(4t+1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac65a6f7-9738-5d33-887a-9ad58c1f5490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444300} :answer-field/id #uuid "afb164f4-0f92-5e1b-9127-b2a957f405bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "076128a8-7495-5bb6-b7b4-2c35cb671771" :question/math-academy-id "q-132071" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4t+1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} &  \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}{8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c78679-4fdc-52dc-a8e7-46e00dcb62f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444271} {:answer/id #uuid "2c36339d-550d-50a9-ac87-d1b79dc3d514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444281} {:answer/id #uuid "4a238725-2bd1-54ac-bbb6-7159963ecd45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444285} {:answer/id #uuid "588ff593-33c8-57cf-b908-2d3cd4e4eda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444287} {:answer/id #uuid "911da293-aa1c-5630-aa8e-6b4e6f105496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c36339d-550d-50a9-ac87-d1b79dc3d514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444281} :answer-field/id #uuid "2840ac15-6a7c-5a69-ab78-2aa96a22daa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d730355-3ecf-557f-807f-e7e1ed97b6ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-165241" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t-2⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+4k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+4k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d37b0ad3-d75a-5e9c-b32f-03d04a5aa5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367916} {:answer/id #uuid "07857a98-a240-5250-8ec4-acfbd5c7ae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367917} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee85e5-b512-5d13-8b8c-1dd0de294bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367918} {:answer/id #uuid "00ecb62a-ae5e-5aed-867f-320853bd4196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367919} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1b9131-e15e-5c67-8056-20b52038f360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d37b0ad3-d75a-5e9c-b32f-03d04a5aa5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367916} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbcea62-bc50-5399-998e-516065a90ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f0173d8-1b20-5423-8c43-633f5b8b0c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-165243" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2t⟩,$  use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k).$*" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =8\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{(2\\sqrt{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{16\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2bacd0f-24fc-559e-9299-4608d1542763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444298} {:answer/id #uuid "a36ef49d-80e1-577c-8d08-f4a49353a82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3({t}^{2}+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444299} {:answer/id #uuid "dadaec8e-26a0-5da2-84a3-c4a2326b68ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444309} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf3cf16-f0ca-5637-9809-9b6a71caec58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444311} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e2e194-e553-5ed9-8cc4-61ffcab44e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({t}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dadaec8e-26a0-5da2-84a3-c4a2326b68ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444309} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb12e737-fb94-52b4-acc6-88a1547c5cff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b5962d7-05b0-532b-8c6d-f094e8fba0fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-165246" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6t,\\,{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =3⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =3\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{4+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $({t}^{2}+2)>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =18\\sqrt{{(-{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =18({t}^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18({t}^{2}+2)}{{(3({t}^{2}+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31064109-55cc-5be1-9a7f-17597c230987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315399} {:answer/id #uuid "33699acb-a73f-5709-9040-a47e293edf3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315419} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc83917-1cfa-542d-8353-d1860b1c223e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315461} {:answer/id #uuid "572ac41e-144e-58a4-9750-8a1cece44b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315622} {:answer/id #uuid "6c73414f-cf97-5567-81bf-1d9361692fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc83917-1cfa-542d-8353-d1860b1c223e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315461} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d73278-34a9-5b7e-8976-1f41a381eee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96d2a067-654d-55aa-b5c4-b48e4bab8c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34202" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨2,\\,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027d08e5-4fb2-5cdf-8e69-94adcf33c27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{({cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444272} {:answer/id #uuid "24f0028a-7323-5cc0-87c2-f487c039cf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444278} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8f11ce-6526-5329-9795-0b707389fea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444288} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc3fac5-5112-57d8-8ccd-d0b4ff66f35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(2{cos}^{2}⁡t+3{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444307} {:answer/id #uuid "e44d4b63-0b17-5d85-b565-88b6b006fe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f0028a-7323-5cc0-87c2-f487c039cf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9607fc20-f180-54be-8b8c-9a4822e7bd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7326acab-616b-5037-877a-7e6bd9629162" :question/math-academy-id "q-34389" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2cos)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce26e03-d563-568e-aa5d-992e2c4b26f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1\\,000|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{|}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444277} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1a067a-8d2b-5397-8bd3-24d1405573aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444291} {:answer/id #uuid "734228d6-96a3-5705-9452-b117125fb933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444292} {:answer/id #uuid "a00913e3-150c-5757-bc29-0491a7b33dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1\\,000|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t{|}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444297} {:answer/id #uuid "cf174ac9-d620-53ea-82b8-3479aa692564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c1a067a-8d2b-5397-8bd3-24d1405573aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444291} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb55b1d-75c9-5fb5-9c67-287204de1c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b9a8797-8d36-57ec-bbd4-59c107c7ab69" :question/math-academy-id "q-64755" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$, as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩=⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{100{cos}^{2}⁡2t}=10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩=⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,0⟩\\times ⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & 0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =(96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{(10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|)^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149c4190-2377-5b9f-8613-d4ec383bf27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348622} {:answer/id #uuid "2a70b37e-c9e6-5017-9c9c-8b865f1fc14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348822} {:answer/id #uuid "7e362f97-df9d-5f58-b60e-586d80ad1d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349602} {:answer/id #uuid "b86d71ae-a0ce-55c6-b718-567342fd92e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350079} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a4511f-9d32-57d5-8f7c-1d5a4003650c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a4511f-9d32-57d5-8f7c-1d5a4003650c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fc46ea-c67a-55ec-aca8-963976aff8ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62f8ada8-0edf-581d-85ea-b6a85fd96890" :question/math-academy-id "q-83402" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(2,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123d29d6-8aaf-5afb-862b-e46ce76281e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444275} {:answer/id #uuid "1b297cdb-aecf-5f48-af66-84c29ddda497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444276} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc57f81-b019-581e-9c4a-3f95a1c41bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444280} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0e8910-4151-5310-9c16-3f2d1d8be9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444282} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a80722-6c7c-5c77-acb3-2b2fa7e23bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b297cdb-aecf-5f48-af66-84c29ddda497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444276} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1859a39-a2db-5a73-b35c-f7fe6ddee607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e89f4c3b-6723-58b1-b21d-dbb9b8ced775" :question/math-academy-id "q-83404" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},{t}^{2},\\,\\,t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3},{t}^{2},\\,\\,t⟩ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(\\frac{2}{3},1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},\\,2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨4,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a6f877-facd-5ea0-8579-e0d7c191bd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444279} {:answer/id #uuid "33ab4b89-4470-5e8b-aee7-5a0fe5da5f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444283} {:answer/id #uuid "54fa053c-1b85-5eca-ba73-3ffa7456b013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444286} {:answer/id #uuid "77770b63-6e34-588d-bcf9-716fc7c40db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444293} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8a20ec-0cfe-5f28-9075-f6395f297291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77770b63-6e34-588d-bcf9-716fc7c40db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444293} :answer-field/id #uuid "998024b6-f7ae-51f5-8648-5d3da46e7c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de097a4f-c54e-5f8e-b869-e4dc2af58fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-83407" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,2t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {e}^{2t}=1 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,4{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-8i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cbc6f5-dd74-546a-b75d-90d4feb0f218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{4(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351624} {:answer/id #uuid "167fbbff-610a-5ffc-98a4-f7a7c22e90c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351763} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7368fb-0ce5-5371-9c5e-757a189fdc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352251} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4ce6e-c86a-57cb-910b-11b5b57f298c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352589} {:answer/id #uuid "94e029dc-a141-5cc4-b20b-d0417f2a0541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "167fbbff-610a-5ffc-98a4-f7a7c22e90c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351763} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a338507-2643-502c-a39c-8708c7b0e849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37bfba69-0cf1-5131-a718-2a25bbc6b8f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86785" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3,\\,3t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t>0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(3)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9(1+{t}^{4})} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =18t\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|18t|=18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{18t}{{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})}^{3}}=\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5c28aa-e40b-5d7d-9621-b891be1115bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444274} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7fddc5-e9f3-50c7-becb-6d8967a0296e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444294} {:answer/id #uuid "823f8f03-83d5-50e3-94a5-43fd37ec5943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444295} {:answer/id #uuid "c975a084-8904-518b-9668-d1ed45bc4672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(36{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444305} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0a86bb-661c-5cb1-b20b-55887d482bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "823f8f03-83d5-50e3-94a5-43fd37ec5943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444295} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4649305-d5dc-5854-97b6-ea90ae323c38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ed8ed9c-cb01-554d-a0b3-14bce9a90d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-86796" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6-3{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,6t-2⟩$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)⟩=⟨-6t,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩=⟨-6,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-6t,0,6⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -6t & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-36\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{36}{{(6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
